  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry weather across\neast central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate clear skies across with plenty of sunshine\nfor east central Florida. Temperatures are seasonable in the upper\n70s to low 80s with comfortable humidity with dew points in the\nupper 50s to mid 60s. Analysis charts show high pressure (~1024mb)\nbuilding to the northwest of Florida over the Mid South. \n\nDry weather is expected to persist tonight with seasonable to\nabove normal lows in the upper 50s to around 70F degrees. Light\nand variable winds combined with a radiation inversion will result\nin the potential for patchy fog tonight along and north of I-4 \n(mainly between 4AM-8AM) with visibility reductions to 3 miles or\nless. Remember to use low beam headlights and slow down if you\nencounter fog.\n\nMonday-Tuesday... A persistent dry weather pattern is expected\nthrough at least early this week as high pressure (~1022mb) builds\nover the Mid-South into Monday and across the Deep South Tuesday.\nLight northeast winds on Monday are expected to shift east-\nsoutheast on Tuesday. High temperatures will be seasonable in the\nlow to mid 80s with seasonable to above normal lows in the upper\n50s to around 70F degrees. \n\nWednesday-Saturday... A weak shortwave is forecast to traverse \nthe Deep South and northern Florida on Wednesday which with \nincreased moisture will result in a gradual increase in rain \nchances mid to late week. Rain chances then increase on Thursday \nand Friday with a cold frontal passage that is forecast Thursday \ninto Friday. Deterministic guidance continues to diverge with two \npotential solutions. The GFS and LREF-ensemble clusters show a \ndeeper low over the southern US with a greater potential for rain \nacross east central Florida. The ECMWF and CMC show a weaker low \nthat is further north across the northeastern US which would \nresult in lower chances Thursday into Friday. \n\nThe NBM still appears to have a low bias in precipitation chances\nWednesday-Friday, therefore, it is likely that rain chances will \nincrease as we get closer to the event with at least scattered \ncoverage. Additionally, one thing to keep in mind is that the \ndynamics are favorable for strong storms Thursday into Friday \n(especially if the GFS is correct), whereas, instability is\nforecast to be lacking. There will be a much better potential for\nstrong storms during the aforementioned time period if instability\nincreases with future model runs. Isolated showers (20% chance) are\nforecast on Friday with isolated to widely scattered showers\n(~20-30% chance) on Thursday. Dry weather is expected behind the \nfront. Seasonable high temperatures and seasonable to above \naverage lows are forecast Wednesday with below normal temperatures\nlate week and into the weekend. The coldest air of the season \nthus far is expected behind the cold front.",
